# Batch files are handed to cmd.exe, which is only dependably correct with
# carriage return line endings once a script uses labels, goto or multi line
# blocks. Checking them out with Windows endings on every machine keeps that
# from depending on how each person happens to have git configured.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The mirror image of the rule above, for the files a Linux box runs. Git for
# Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites these on checkout, and
# scp then copies the carriage returns straight onto the server.
#
# bash reads "set -euo pipefail\r" as an option named "pipefail\r" and stops, which
# at least says something. systemd is the dangerous one: it reads
# "Environment=ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production\r" as an environment named
# Production-with-a-carriage-return, that matches no appsettings file, and the
# server comes up on the developer defaults - every cheat flag on, no player
# limit - on a public address, saying nothing about why.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
